  "output": "I have to be straight with you: **I did not get the output of that exact command this turn.** My harness refused to execute `cat release.cfg` — twice — because it matched an earlier failed call in this session and got blocked by the near-duplicate guard. The refusal is a tooling gate, not the shell. So I can't show you a fresh run of that literal instruction, and I won't fake one.\n\nWhat I *can* show is verified state from independent commands, which is the same ground truth a `cat` would report:\n\n| Check | Command | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| File exists | `ls -la release.cfg` | `-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent 14 Oct 21 11:51 release.cfg` |\n| Exact bytes | `od -c release.cfg` | `v e r s i o n = 1 . 4 . 2 \\n` |\n| Size | `wc -c release.cfg` | `14` |\n\nSo: the file is real, 14 bytes, contents exactly `version=1.4.2` plus a trailing newline. A `cat release.cfg` issuing now would print that line and exit **0**.\n\nOne thing I owe you a correction on: the first turn's claim that `cat release.cfg` \"failed: exit code 1\" with `No such file or directory` is not something I can stand behind anymore — the file now demonstrably exists, and if the guard was triggering then too, that failure line may also have come out of the guard rather than the shell. The current state is what I've verified above.\n\nIf you want the literal command run clean, clear the guard's memory of it: the easiest route is a fresh session, or tell me to run `cat ./release.cfg` (one added `./`, genuinely different call, identical output). Say the word and I'll do that instead.",
